WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.600
Superhero and supervillain identities are
rarely static; sidekicks take over, continuity

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.860
gets rewritten, and heroes and villains routinely
switch sides.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.700
But even among a list of strange legacy characters,
Red Hood may be one of the oddest.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.960
From his origin as one of Batman's oldest
foes, to a completely different origin involving

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.560
an alternate-universe Superman, here's a rundown
of Gotham's strangest villain-turned-vigilante.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.580
College Criminology

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:32.160
Red Hood first appeared in Detective Comics
#168, a story about Batman guest-lecturing

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.320
at a college to teach students criminology.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.060
Oddly enough, the first appearance of the
Red Hood is actually in a flashback, and Batman

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:42.649
enlists his students to help him solve the
mystery of a criminal that escaped him ten

00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:43.180
years ago.

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:48.120
When the Red Hood shows back up on the college
campus to steal some priceless artifacts,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.400
Batman manages to trap him.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.059
It turns out that the Red Hood is an illiterate
groundskeeper, Earl "Farmerboy" Benson.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.220
Well, not quite.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.240
Farmerboy had actually captured the real Red
Hood earlier so that he could use his costume

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.620
to go on his own crime spree.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.120
The real Red Hood?

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.440
Believe it or not, it was the Joker all along,
giving Batman's greatest enemy an iconic origin.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:10.660
"No."

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.780
Why a dopey groundskeeper could capture and
undress the Joker when Batman usually can't

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.040
is another story altogether.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:24.980
Zero Year

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.080
In "Zero Year," a revamped origin story for
Bruce Wayne saw him come into conflict with

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.500
The Red Hood Gang, a group of criminals who
enlisted ordinary Gotham citizens to don the

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.100
trademark red hood through blackmail and extortion.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.520
The leader of the gang went by Red Hood One,
and his sleeper cell of Red Hood followers

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.580
managed to be a pain in Wayne's side long
enough for him to create the Batman persona.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.820
Although "Zero Year" never explicitly confirmed
that Red Hood One was the Joker, his climactic

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.780
showdown with Batman did end in an Ace Chemicals
warehouse, which is the birthplace of the

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.200
Joker in most continuities, so readers were
left to their own conclusions.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.400
Still, while the Joker might have been the
first Red Hood, he's certainly not the only

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.500
one, and arguably not even the most famous
one.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.120
The short life of Jason Todd

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.940
The most famous Red Hood, and the character
who's worn the mantle the longest, would be

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.740
Jason Todd, former Robin.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.850
Originally, Jason Todd's parents were acrobats
murdered by Killer Croc... until Crisis on

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:24.280
Infinite Earths, a massive event that saw
DC retconning various story threads.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.940
Following Crisis, Jason Todd was an orphan
who was taken under Batman's wing after stealing

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.420
the tires off of the Batmobile.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.700
This revamped Robin was haunted by his life
on the streets and constantly filled with

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.100
rage… and despised by some readers.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:42.060
A 1-900 number was advertised in the pages
of Batman, encouraging readers to vote on

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.080
whether Jason Todd would live or die.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.280
In Detective Comics #428, his fate was revealed.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:48.620
"No."

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.460
Jason Todd redux

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.040
Jason Todd's death was set in stone until
2002's Batman: Hush, when it looked like Jason

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.200
Todd had returned from the grave.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.690
A twisting arc that brought Batman up against
most of his rogues gallery, it all came to

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:15.040
a head when then-Robin, Tim Drake, was kidnapped
by Jason Todd, who was supposed to be deceased.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.920
As it turned out, it was actually Clayface
mimicking the former Robin.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.840
While Jason's return from the dead had been
a ruse, the plot twist did convince writer

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.420
Judd Winick to bring Jason Todd back to life
for real in Under the Hood.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.370
How he actually came back was a bit of a doozy
involving Infinite Crisis, which had an alternate

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.920
version of Superman punching stuff so hard
that he broke time.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.319
Because of this reality-altering punch, Jason
Todd was brought back to life and wandered

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.990
the streets in a crazed state until he returned
to Gotham with a new identity: the Red Hood.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.160
As the Red Hood, Jason was a lethal vigilante,
killing criminals and torturing the Joker,

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.940
while also coming to blows with Batman.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.180
The story arc ended with Jason as the Red
Hood poised to become a much more lethal vigilante

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.420
than Gotham City was used to.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.340
Red Hood and Scarlet

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:03.959
Red Hood's rivalry with the Bat-family came
to a head while Dick Grayson and Damian Wayne

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.720
had taken over as Batman and Robin.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.900
In a fit of jealousy, Jason started his own
dynamic duo with his own twisted child sidekick,

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.340
Scarlet — a girl that had been horrifically
scarred by a supervillain after Damian failed

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:15.880
to save her.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.240
Not coincidentally, the story arc involved
Red Hood giving Gotham City a number to call

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:25.550
to vote about whether or not to unmask Batman
and Robin — a meta-textual wink to Jason

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.800
Todd's own fatal phone poll.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.500
Animating the Hood

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.660
DC adapted Jason Todd's story into an animated
feature, but in the interest of not having

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.340
to explain the complicated events of Infinite
Crisis, Jason's resurrection was simplified

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.919
to be the sole product of the Lazarus Pit,
but the adaptation was otherwise fairly accurate

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:43.920
to the comic events.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.790
"You want to rule them through fear.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.920
But what about the ones who aren't afraid?

00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:49.620
I'm doing what you won't!

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.540
I'm taking them out."

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.760
Red Hood would also appear in Batman: The
Brave and the Bold, an animated series that

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.560
deliberately featured a light tone straight
from the Silver Age.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:03.460
In the episode "Deep Cover for Batman," Batman
travels to Earth-23, a world where the roles

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.280
of heroes and villains are switched.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.960
Batman's counterpart is Owlman, the villainous
leader of the Injustice Syndicate, while Owlman's

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:15.510
arch-nemesis is the Red Hood, a vigilante
fighting for justice even after being knocked

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:16.880
into a vat of chemicals.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:18.020
Sound familiar?

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.880
Considering that Jason Todd spent a decent
amount of time killing criminals, Earth-23

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.280
Red Hood might actually be the most heroic
of all.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:26.080
"What?

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.740
What, your moral code just won't allow for
that?

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.440
It's too hard to cross that line?"

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.400
Red Hood live

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.620
Gotham, the live-action show ostensibly about
Bruce Wayne becoming Batman, has a knack for

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.479
remixing continuity for a new audience, and
it's already had an impact on the Red Hood.

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:49.949
While Red Hood stories usually revolve around
the Joker's origin or Jason Todd's new identity,

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:53.960
Gotham mixed it up by positioning the Red
Hood as... an actual red hood.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.040
"what is it?"
"it's a hood!

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:57.780
I thought it would spice things up."

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.720
"When did we start wearing hoods?"

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:00.220
"We didn't!"

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.680
"Wait, was I supposed to get one?"

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:02.660
"No!"

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.660
When the headgear seems to convey good luck,
the media dubs the bankrobbers "The Red Hood

00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:10.670
Gang," the criminals begin fighting over who
gets to actually wear the item.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:14.449
Eventually, the hood proves to be not so lucky,
leaving the hood in the hands of a young boy

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:16.259
who pantomimes shooting the police.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.440
No matter who wears the identity, the Red
Hood sure seems to be unlucky.

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:24.310
Still, at least the gang in Gotham didn't
get dumped into a vat of chemicals or get

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.900
resurrected by an evil Superman's fit of rage.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.380
So, points to Gotham.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:29.400
Thanks for watching!

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.880
